WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.501
-Welcome back.
-Thank you so much.

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.200
-Welcome back, welcome back.
-Thank you so much.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:06.334 --> 00:00:07.667
Thank you for being here.

00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:09.434
It means a lot to me.
Thank you so much for coming.

00:00:09.434 --> 00:00:12.501
You know I love you and you've
been a great guest on the show.

00:00:12.501 --> 00:00:17.100
And I was a little late to the
party by reading "Greenlights"

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.968
and I called you up,
I think it was last year.

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:22.000
And I said, "Hey, dude,
I really love 'Greenlights.'

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.300
Congratulations. This is great."

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.767
And you go, "It's been out
two years," and I go --

00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:27.133
-You got to.
-But I got --

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:29.667
It is unbelievable. And I told
you the story already,

00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:31.634
so I don't want to bore you.
But I read it and I loved it.

00:00:31.634 --> 00:00:33.334
I couldn't put it down.
And then my friend said,

00:00:33.334 --> 00:00:36.234
"Did you do the audible book?"
I go, "No, I read it."

00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:38.167
And they go,
"Yeah, you got to do the audible

00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:40.701
because it's Matthew talking,
reading the book."

00:00:40.701 --> 00:00:41.901
I go, "You're kidding me."

00:00:41.901 --> 00:00:44.367
So I spent probably
24 hours of my life

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:45.968
with you
and you didn't know about it,

00:00:45.968 --> 00:00:48.534
and it was worth every second.
-We did alright?

00:00:48.534 --> 00:00:52.667
-I loved it so much.
Can you say alright anymore?

00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:55.934
-Oh, I can say alright.
-Okay, good. I just don't know.

00:00:55.934 --> 00:00:57.767
-People ask me all the time.
"Do you get tired of that?"

00:00:57.767 --> 00:00:59.367
I said, "No.
It's the first three words

00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:02.901
I ever said on film 32 years ago
when it precedes me.

00:01:02.901 --> 00:01:04.567
I thought I might just
have a one-night hobby.

00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:06.400
It turned into a career.
Please say it."

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.868
-[ Laughs ] Yeah, that story
is a great story.

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:11.767
It's in this book, by the way,
and congrats on the success.

00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:14.133
I think it was number one
for 98 weeks or something.

00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:16.968
Almost 100 weeks.
-Moving up on 100. Yeah.

00:01:16.968 --> 00:01:18.734
I had no idea that it was

00:01:18.734 --> 00:01:21.367
going to hit the main vein
like it did and has,

00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:22.901
but I've enjoyed
the conversations

00:01:22.901 --> 00:01:24.767
with everybody around the world
that I've run into

00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:26.634
that have told me
how that book has affected them.

00:01:26.634 --> 00:01:28.167
So thank you.
It's my pleasure.

00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:31.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:31.434 --> 00:01:33.734
-Do you get nervous
when you think about

00:01:33.734 --> 00:01:35.634
maybe doing a book like this?
Do you go like, "I don't know

00:01:35.634 --> 00:01:36.667
if I want to be this person,

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:38.901
I want to be this honest?"
-Right.

00:01:38.901 --> 00:01:41.400
-Do publicists get involved
and they go, "Matthew, do not --

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.400
don't tell that story."

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:43.667
-No, when I went to write it --

00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:45.300
I mean, look,
I didn't ask permission

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.667
about what stories
to put on there,

00:01:46.667 --> 00:01:48.267
although in post,
I don't know --

00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:49.334
my publicist is out there.

00:01:49.334 --> 00:01:50.934
I think we did alright.

00:01:50.934 --> 00:01:53.734
I told personal stories,

00:01:53.734 --> 00:01:57.501
but I tried to tell them
in a way that anyone -- age,

00:01:57.501 --> 00:01:59.033
female, male, whatever --
could understand them

00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:01.367
and see themselves in my story.
And that's been the main thing

00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:03.033
I've heard from people
around the world going,

00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:04.367
"Hey, I've been
in that scenario

00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:06.601
and I chose to do something
different than you did.

00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:09.167
I'm going to try what you did"
or "I've been in that scenario

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:10.901
and I screwed up like you did,
McConaughey."

00:02:10.901 --> 00:02:13.834
You know? So they're seeing me
and them.

00:02:13.834 --> 00:02:15.901
-And talking about your mom
and dad and all that stuff.

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:19.200
But they're so interesting
and it's so honest in here.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.734
And I go, "Oh, that's real."

00:02:20.734 --> 00:02:22.701
This book is so real
and I love that.

00:02:22.701 --> 00:02:26.300
And then kind of you
could see

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.400
the seeds of "Just Because" in
"Greenlights"

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.067
because this tree house here,

00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:34.100
there's a story behind
this tree house.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:35.601
-I had a 13-story tree house

00:02:35.601 --> 00:02:38.234
that I write about
in "Greenlights" that I found

00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:40.767
a hardware construction store
out in the middle of the woods,

00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:43.868
and I came across it,
waited till they got off work,

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:45.300
got the wire cutters,
cut through the fence

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.067
and snuck through
and started stealing lumber

00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:49.701
and would take it
to this one big Ponderosa pine

00:02:49.701 --> 00:02:52.734
in the backwoods where I lived.
And I had all the wood

00:02:52.734 --> 00:02:54.200
to make the most incredible
tree house in the world.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.534
I made a 13-story tree house
over an entire summer.

00:02:58.534 --> 00:03:00.267
And that's why
that's on the cover.

00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:01.667
A tree house,
I think, is a symbol

00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:03.868
for all of us in our youth.
It's the first place for me

00:03:03.868 --> 00:03:06.367
where I got to go
and have my private thoughts,

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:08.567
write down my private things,
have my things that I was like,

00:03:08.567 --> 00:03:10.567
"No one else can see that
if I don't want them to.

00:03:10.567 --> 00:03:13.501
Only people in my tree house is
who I invite to my tree house."

00:03:13.501 --> 00:03:15.567
So that's what "Just Because"
it's on the cover.

00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:17.300
-And so you write this

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.601
and you go,
"Okay, let's give it a shot.

00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:22.100
I'll release
this children's book."

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.400
Did you ever think you would
ever write a children's book?

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.634
I mean, you're a great dad.
-Ehhh.

00:03:26.634 --> 00:03:27.934
I never really thought about it.

00:03:27.934 --> 00:03:30.601
Look, this came to me
in a little ditty of a dream

00:03:30.601 --> 00:03:33.367
one night, and I woke up at 2:30
with this little hook.

00:03:33.367 --> 00:03:35.067
You know, just because
they threw the dog

00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:36.801
don't mean that it stuck,
da-da-da.

00:03:36.801 --> 00:03:39.534
Just because I got skills
don't mean there is no luck.

00:03:39.534 --> 00:03:41.033
And I woke up
and it was a good dream.

00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:43.300
And as you do in good dreams,
you don't want them to end.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.267
You try to keep the rhythm
of the thought, right?

00:03:45.267 --> 00:03:47.934
I get up and I write...
[ Scatting ]

00:03:47.934 --> 00:03:49.100
And I write all this stuff down.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.467
I write about 200 couplets,

00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:52.367
30 of which
made it into this version

00:03:52.367 --> 00:03:55.300
'cause this is a younger --
younger person version.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.901
And I looked at and I
found those 30. I was like,

00:03:56.901 --> 00:03:59.801
"Hey, this is some stuff that
my children are going through.

00:03:59.801 --> 00:04:02.667
This is some stuff that I hear
my kids' friends talking about

00:04:02.667 --> 00:04:05.200
they're going though. This is
some stuff that I hear parents

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:06.234
or their friends of mine saying,

00:04:06.234 --> 00:04:07.167
'My kids are going through
this.'"

00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:08.934
So that's what's in the book.

00:04:08.934 --> 00:04:12.000
-I dig it, man. I love it.
This is so up my alley.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.434
This is like -- Even if I didn't
have kids, I would have this.

00:04:15.434 --> 00:04:16.734
-Right on.
-It really is.

00:04:16.734 --> 00:04:18.367
It's one of those, like,

00:04:18.367 --> 00:04:21.334
kind of "Giving Tree"
kind of ideas for me where I go,

00:04:21.334 --> 00:04:24.434
"I want to remind myself
of all these things."

00:04:24.434 --> 00:04:26.767
What do your kids think
of the book?

00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:28.100
-The kids dig it.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:31.267
Look, look, I've got --
My daughter is very visual.

00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:33.501
She loves the illustrations,
which Renée Kurilla did,

00:04:33.501 --> 00:04:36.067
who's in the audience today.
-Fantastic illustrations.

00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:37.901
-Yeah.
-Great job. So cute.

00:04:37.901 --> 00:04:40.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:40.634 --> 00:04:43.367
-My eldest and I have talked
about similar scenarios

00:04:43.367 --> 00:04:45.434
that he's going through now
in his teenage years.

00:04:45.434 --> 00:04:46.634
My youngest is still sort of

00:04:46.634 --> 00:04:48.200
holding out to tell me
if he likes it or not,

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.567
but that's just
kind of his character.

00:04:49.567 --> 00:04:51.234
[ Laughter ]

00:04:51.234 --> 00:04:55.534
-I like the dedication
at the end of the book there.

00:04:55.534 --> 00:04:58.634
Would you mind reading it?
-What's it say?

00:04:58.634 --> 00:05:02.434
"To my kids, your kids,
and the kid in all of us,

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:06.701
we're all as young
as we're ever going to be.

00:05:06.701 --> 00:05:09.734
So let's just keep learning.
McConaughey." Yeah.

00:05:09.734 --> 00:05:12.100
-That's it. That's exactly it.
-Isn't that true?

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:13.801 --> 00:05:16.634
We're a young as we're
ever going to be right now.

00:05:16.634 --> 00:05:18.000
-That's so true.
-Oh, just got older.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.801
-We just --
-Oh, we just got older.

00:05:19.801 --> 00:05:21.467
-Oh, we just got older.
-Oh, we just got older.

00:05:21.467 --> 00:05:24.901
-Yeah, exactly.
Also, by the way,

00:05:24.901 --> 00:05:26.734
I'm happy
you're here in New York City

00:05:26.734 --> 00:05:27.934
'cause last time you were here,

00:05:27.934 --> 00:05:29.534
you went to
the Empire State Building.

00:05:29.534 --> 00:05:31.367
-Yes.
-To talk about "Just Because."

00:05:31.367 --> 00:05:32.667
Right?
-This was wild.

00:05:32.667 --> 00:05:35.033
-And then they surprise you.
I love this.

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:36.567
What was going
through your head?

00:05:36.567 --> 00:05:38.033
You were there with your wife.

00:05:38.033 --> 00:05:39.868
-So, I didn't even know I was
in the Empire State Building.

00:05:39.868 --> 00:05:41.067
I'm doing the press tour
for the book.

00:05:41.067 --> 00:05:42.334
I'm pointing to shoot.
"Tell me where to go.

00:05:42.334 --> 00:05:43.701
My head's down.
Tell me who to talk to."

00:05:43.701 --> 00:05:44.968
And I walk in this building.

00:05:44.968 --> 00:05:46.200
I think I'm going to go
into another interview.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:49.000
There's my wife with
a whole bunch of cameramen

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.701
and camerawomen and my journal,
and she says, "Read this."

00:05:51.701 --> 00:05:53.901
And I'm like, "What's going on?"
And I read it and it says,

00:05:53.901 --> 00:05:55.634
"I wrote 'Greenlights'
three years ago.

00:05:55.634 --> 00:05:57.133
Little did I know
that we'd be coming up on

00:05:57.133 --> 00:05:59.167
100 weeks
on the best-seller list.

00:05:59.167 --> 00:06:01.567
And congratulations to that.'
The Empire State Building

00:06:01.567 --> 00:06:03.133
is lighting itself
green tonight."

00:06:03.133 --> 00:06:04.534
And I was like...

00:06:04.534 --> 00:06:06.167
They're like,
"Yeah, you're in it."

00:06:06.167 --> 00:06:08.767
And we went upstairs and...
-Look at this.

00:06:08.767 --> 00:06:10.901
-Pulled the lever
and this turned on green

00:06:10.901 --> 00:06:12.968
that night in New York City
for a doggone...

00:06:12.968 --> 00:06:14.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:14.734 --> 00:06:15.934
-Come on!
-That's one of those things

00:06:15.934 --> 00:06:18.400
that's like on your bucket list,

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.868
but you don't even know
to put it on your bucket list.

00:06:20.868 --> 00:06:25.200
-That's so cool. I'm so happy.
But congrats on both books,

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.767
"Greenlights" and "Just Because"
right here right now,

00:06:27.767 --> 00:06:30.801
everybody, pick it up
wherever books are sold.

00:06:30.801 --> 00:06:32.267
We're going to do something fun
with the book

00:06:32.267 --> 00:06:34.467
and Matthew McConaughey,
everybody, come on back.

